{"name":"Wall angels","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/St_Michael_%5E_All_Angels%2C_Stonebridge_-_Wall_monument_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4863496.jpg/960px-St_Michael_%5E_All_Angels%2C_Stonebridge_-_Wall_monument_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4863496.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Michael_%5E_All_Angels,_Stonebridge_-_Wall_monument_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4863496.jpg","caption":"St Michael ^ All Angels, Stonebridge - Wall monument - geograph.org.uk - 4863496","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Wall angels have you stand with your back against a wall and slide your arms up and down while keeping head, upper back, hips, elbows and hands in contact. People use them to open up rounded shoulders from desk work and stand straighter.","Fitness sources say the movement stretches the chest while engaging the rhomboids, trapezius, serratus anterior and rotator cuff and encouraging thoracic extension. It is common in physical therapy, Pilates and general fitness; no published trial of wall angels for posture or appearance was located."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People do a few slow sets in front of a wall as a warm-up or daily posture routine, often alongside chest stretches and rowing movements. Range can be scaled down if the arms will not stay on the wall."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["It needs only a wall, and advice is to reduce range if pain occurs. No harms are documented in the sources reviewed."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/St_Michael_%5E_All_Angels%2C_Stonebridge_-_Wall_monument_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4863496.jpg/960px-St_Michael_%5E_All_Angels%2C_Stonebridge_-_Wall_monument_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4863496.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.